Eleven organizations compromised in just 26 seconds.
That’s how quickly an AI-assisted campaign compromised multiple organizations, according to research cited in the Nov. 16 Google Threat Intelligence Group report, which also documented Google’s first observation of threat actors using AI to both find and exploit software flaws without human intervention.
A patch can be ready to install and still sit in a queue until the next maintenance window. If attackers are already targeting an internet-facing system, that wait can leave it exposed. IT teams need to know which fixes they can push ahead of schedule and who can isolate a compromised machine when an alert comes in.
“Enterprises are going to be under enormous pressure to speed up their patch cycles in the near future,” John Hultquist, chief analyst at Google Threat Intelligence Group, said in a blog post.

